<TopicPage slug="subjects/psychology/expert/clinical-practice-and-ethics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Clinical practice is <strong>where psychological science meets a person in distress</strong> —
    and the meeting is governed by evidence, ethics and a working relationship. This lesson
    covers what actually happens in the room, what predicts success, and the ethical frame that
    makes the whole enterprise safe enough to work.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="alliance, evidence streams, the dodo-bird question, and the guardrails" widget="equationStep" config={clinCfg} />

  <h2>The professional realities</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Measurement-based care:</strong> tracking symptoms every session (PHQ-9, GAD-7) doubles improvement rates — feedback catches stagnation early. The data-science habit, clinically applied.</li>
    <li><strong>Cultural competence is evidence-based:</strong> outcomes improve when therapy matches clients' cultural frames and languages — not politeness, effect size.</li>
    <li><strong>Therapist drift is real:</strong> practitioners stray from proven protocols over time; supervision, measurement and deliberate practice are the countermeasures. Experts need feedback too (Ericsson's deliberate practice).</li>
    <li><strong>Burnout is systemic:</strong> ~20–40% of clinicians report burnout; caseloads, trauma exposure and administrative load are structural causes. Sustainable care requires sustainable carers — a systems problem (the medicine ladder's health-systems lesson, applied).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Wampold & Imel, The Great Psychotherapy Debate; Lambert, Bergin and Garfield's Handbook of Psychotherapy; APA Ethical Principles.</p>
</TopicPage>
